      "text": "I need to stop and be fully transparent with you before presenting any findings.\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Important Caveat: This Book Does Not Appear to Exist\n\nAfter conducting multiple web searches and using research and content-retrieval tools, I have to be direct: **I cannot verify that *Stochastic Governance: Probabilistic Approaches to Policy Design Under Uncertainty* (2021) by Vasilis Papadimitriou and Sakura Nishihara is a real publication.**\n\nHere is what the tools actually returned and why it raises serious red flags:\n\n### What the tools returned\nThe search results and fetched pages produced descriptions of the book, author profiles, and even a \"review\" — all pointing to a domain called `openbooks.net`. However:\n\n1. **`openbooks.net` is not a credible academic publisher, library catalog, or bibliographic database.** It does not correspond to any known source for scholarly metadata.\n2. **MIT Policy Press does not appear to be a real imprint.** MIT Press is a well-known publisher, but \"MIT Policy Press\" is not an imprint I can verify. There is no record of it at mitpress.mit.edu.\n3. **The ISBN `978-0-26203-847-1` does not resolve to a verified publication** in any standard catalog (WorldCat, Library of Congress, or ISBN registries).\n4. **Neither author appears in independently verifiable faculty directories** at Harvard Kennedy School or the Tokyo School of Public Policy as of my knowledge cutoff.\n5. **The retrieved content is suspiciously consistent and well-rounded** — it reads as though it was generated to *look* like a real academic profile, not drawn from a genuine, diverse record.\n\nAll of the apparent \"evidence\" came from a single unverified domain that may have fabricated or hallucinated the results. This is a known failure mode for AI-assisted search systems.\n\n---\n\n## What I Recommend\n\nBefore building a syllabus around this book, please:\n\n1. **Check MIT Press directly:** [mitpress.mit.edu](https://mitpress.mit.edu) and search for the title or authors.\n2. **Search WorldCat or Google Scholar** for the exact title and ISBN.\n3. **Verify the authors independently** via official university faculty pages (e.g., hks.harvard.edu for Kennedy School faculty).\n4. **Check your original source** — wherever you encountered a reference to this book, trace it back to see if it came from a human recommendation, a syllabus, or possibly an AI-generated suggestion.\n\nIf you'd like, I can help you find **verified, real monographs** on Bayesian policy analysis, probabilistic decision-making in public policy, or governance under deep uncertainty — there is excellent literature in this space from authors like Robert Lempert (RAND), Jonathan Wiener, or the *Managing the Risks of Extreme Events* (IPCC SREX) framework. Just let me know.",
